--TEST--
`Core\Arr::overlayDeep` combines two arrays nested far deeper than any native call stack, because its walk carries the nesting on the heap — spec § 2, `rule:types/array-combination` and `rule:programs/memory-priority`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The nesting depth of an argument is the caller's data: a request that
// reaches this member with two configuration trees decides how far down it is
// walked. A frame per level would make that a reach into this process's own
// stack, which ends the worker and every other request on it rather than the
// one that asked — `rule:programs/memory-priority` puts the bound on the
// request's memory ceiling instead, and this depth is what asserts the walk
// is not the stack's.
class Build {
    public static function nest(int $depth, string $leaf, string $mark): array<mixed> {
        array<mixed> $node = [$leaf => $mark];
        int $d = 0;
        while ($d < $depth) {
            $node = ["next" => $node];
            $d = $d + 1;
        }
        return $node;
    }
}

// Two towers of one hundred thousand maps. Both sides hold a map under
// `next` at every level, which is `rule:types/array-combination`'s test for recursing rather
// than replacing, so the walk goes all the way to the bottom.
array<mixed> $base = Build::nest(100000, "leaf", "base");
array<mixed> $layer = Build::nest(100000, "mark", "layer");
array<mixed> $combined = Core\Arr::overlayDeep($base, $layer);

// The depth of the result is the first half of the claim.
array<mixed> $node = $combined;
int $levels = 0;
while (Core\Arr::hasKey($node, "next")) {
    $node = $node["next"] as array<mixed>;
    $levels = $levels + 1;
}
echo $levels, "\n";

// The second half, and what tells a combination from a replacement: the
// bottom map carries the key from each side. A member that had replaced
// wholesale anywhere on the way down would print the layer's key alone.
echo Core\Json::encode($node), "\n";
--EXPECT--
100000
{"leaf":"base","mark":"layer"}
